const BRIEF_TEXT = `<!-- tdag-page:1 -->

# Why Saved Articles Stay Unread

*A short brief on digital hoarding and the collector's fallacy*

## 1. The phenomenon

Read-it-later services report that most saved items are never reopened. Across published usage studies, the share of saved articles that get a second visit is small, and completion rates are smaller still. The archive grows; the reading does not.

## 2. The collector's fallacy

Collecting produces an immediate sense of progress while the understanding it promises is deferred. Saving a text feels like a first step of reading it, but no part of the reading has happened. The act of saving is mistaken for the act of learning.

<!-- tdag-page:2 -->

## 3. Why it persists

The costs are asymmetric: saving takes one second and pays out a small reward now; reading takes an hour and pays out later. Habits follow the cheaper loop, so libraries inflate by default.

## 4. What helps

Interventions that work move processing to the moment of capture: write one line in your own words about why the item matters, and schedule a review pass instead of trusting the archive to resurface it. A smaller library that gets processed beats a complete one that does not.`;

